## 1. Sidecar Pattern

A Sidecar is a secondary container that runs inside the same Pod along with the main application container.

- Main container runs the application.
- Sidecar container provides additional support to the main application.
- Both containers share the same Pod environment.
- Sidecars are commonly used for logging, monitoring and metrics.

### Sidecar Architecture

```text
Pod
│
├── Main Container
│      Application
│
└── Sidecar Container
       Logging / Monitoring
```

### Different Container Patterns

| Pattern | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Ambassador | Proxy communication |
| Adapter | Transform input/output |
| Sidecar | Add helper functionality |
| Init Sidecar | Initialization |
| Service Mesh Sidecar | Traffic and security management |

### Example: Log Aggregation Using Sidecar

The main application writes logs into a shared volume. The sidecar container uses NGINX to serve those logs.

Create `sidecar.yml`.

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: log-aggregator-pod
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app-container
      image: alpine
      command: ["/bin/sh", "-c"]
      args: ["while true; do date >> /var/log/app.log; sleep 5; done"]
      volumeMounts:
        - name: shared-logs
          mountPath: /var/log

    - name: sidecar-container
      image: nginx
      volumeMounts:
        - name: shared-logs
          mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html

  volumes:
    - name: shared-logs
      emptyDir: {}
```

### Commands

```bash
kubectl apply -f sidecar.yml

kubectl get pods

kubectl describe pods log-aggregator-pod

kubectl exec -it log-aggregator-pod -c sidecar-container -- sh

cd /usr/share/nginx/html

cat app.log

kubectl delete -f sidecar.yml
```

**Important:** The application container generates `app.log`, and the sidecar container accesses the same file through the shared volume.

`emptyDir` is temporary storage that exists for the lifetime of the Pod.

