## 2. Init Containers

An Init Container performs initialization work before the main application container starts.

- It executes the initialization task first.
- After successful completion, the main application container starts.
- It is useful when an application depends on another service.

### Example

Create `init.yml`.

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: myapp-pod
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: MyApp
spec:
  containers:
    - name: myapp-container
      image: busybox:1.28
      env:
        - name: FIRSTNAME
          value: "REYAZ"
      command: ['sh', '-c', 'echo The app is running! && sleep 3600']

  initContainers:
    - name: init-myservice
      image: busybox:1.28
      command: ['sh', '-c']
      args:
        - 'until nslookup ib-service.default.svc.cluster.local; do echo waiting for ib-service; sleep 2; done'
```

### Commands

```bash
kubectl create -f init.yml

kubectl get pods

kubectl logs pod/myapp-pod -c init-myservice
```

### Sidecar vs Init Container

| Sidecar | Init Container |
|---|---|
| Runs continuously | Runs once and completes |
| Supports the main application | Prepares the environment |
| Runs alongside the application | Completes before the application starts |

