## 3. Ingress Controller

Ingress helps expose applications running inside a Kubernetes cluster through HTTP and HTTPS routes.

### Main Features

- Host-based routing
- Path-based routing
- Load balancing
- SSL/TLS termination
- External application access

### Host-Based Routing

Routes traffic based on the hostname.

Examples:

```text
boom.com
web.boom.com
admin.boom.com
```

### Path-Based Routing

Routes traffic based on the URL path.

Examples:

```text
boom.com/hello
boom.com/admin
paytm.com/movies
paytm.com/recharge
```

### Why Do We Need Ingress?

Services such as ClusterIP, NodePort and LoadBalancer provide different ways to expose applications.

Ingress provides additional HTTP/HTTPS routing based on hostnames and paths.

### Install NGINX Ingress Controller

```bash
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/ingress-nginx/controller-v1.3.0/deploy/static/provider/cloud/deploy.yaml
```

Check the resources:

```bash
kubectl get pods
kubectl get deploy
kubectl get svc
kubectl get ingress
```

### NGINX and HTTPD Applications

In the practical lab, two applications were deployed:

- NGINX application
- Apache HTTPD application

Both applications use ClusterIP Services.

### Ingress YAML

Create `ingress.yml`.

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: k8s-ingress
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "false"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/use-regex: "true"
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /$2

spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  rules:
    - http:
        paths:
          - path: /nginx(/|$)(.*)
            pathType: ImplementationSpecific
            backend:
              service:
                name: nginx
                port:
                  number: 80

          - path: /httpd(/|$)(.*)
            pathType: ImplementationSpecific
            backend:
              service:
                name: httpd
                port:
                  number: 80

          - path: /(.*)
            pathType: ImplementationSpecific
            backend:
              service:
                name: nginx
                port:
                  number: 80
```

### Apply YAML Files

```bash
kubectl apply -f nginx.yml

kubectl apply -f httpd.yml

kubectl apply -f ingress.yml

kubectl get deploy

kubectl get svc

kubectl get pods

kubectl get ingress
```

Check the Ingress Controller LoadBalancer:

```bash
kubectl get services ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx
```

After the external load balancer is provisioned, access:

```text
http://<ELB>/nginx

http://<ELB>/httpd
```

### Important Ingress Annotations

| Annotation | Purpose |
|---|---|
| ssl-redirect | Controls HTTP-to-HTTPS redirection |
| use-regex | Enables regular-expression path matching |
| rewrite-target | Rewrites the incoming URL path |

**Note:** The controller installation version and annotations should be checked for compatibility before using them in a new cluster.

